<h2 id="not-medical-or-health-advice">1. Not Medical or Health Advice</h2>
    <div class="lp-card">
      <p>Nothing on this Site — including body shape results, waist-to-hip ratio (WHR), waist-to-height ratio (WHtR), BMI figures, or any accompanying commentary — constitutes medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment. Our content should never replace consultation with a qualified physician, dietitian, or other healthcare professional. Always seek the advice of a qualified health provider with any questions you may have regarding a medical condition, weight, body composition, or fertility.</p>
    </div>
    <p>Ratios such as WHR and WHtR are population-level research indicators discussed in published studies; they are not individual diagnostic tools, and a single result from our calculators should never be used to self-diagnose or make health decisions without professional guidance.</p>

    <h2 id="not-a-substitute-for-professional-fitting-or-styling-advice">2. Not a Substitute for Professional Fitting or Styling Advice</h2>
    <p>Body shape classifications and styling suggestions on this Site are general guidance based on documented proportional formulas. They are not a substitute for a professional tailor, stylist, or fitting consultation, and individual results and preferences will vary.</p>

    <h2 id="accuracy-of-content">3. Accuracy of Content</h2>
    <p>We make reasonable efforts to ensure the formulas, statistics, and research citations on this Site are accurate and sourced from credible references at the time of publication. However, research findings, cited statistics, and official guidance (such as WHO thresholds or military fitness standards) can change over time. We do not guarantee that all information remains current, complete, or error-free, and we recommend verifying any figure that is critical to your decision-making against the original source.</p>

    <h2 id="calculator-accuracy">4. Calculator Accuracy</h2>
    <p>Our calculators are only as accurate as the measurements entered into them. Small measurement errors — from technique, tape tension, or posture — can shift a result, particularly near a category boundary. See our <a href="/body-measurements/">measurement guide</a> for technique tips, but ultimately the responsibility for measurement accuracy rests with the user. The <a href="/body-visualizer/">Body Visualizer</a> draws an illustrative figure from the numbers you enter; it is not a body scan or a prediction of how you will look, and its BMI and body-fat figures are estimates.</p>

    <h2 id="external-links">5. External Links</h2>
